---
import { icons } from '../lib/icons';
import { site } from '../lib/site';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What happens to my Windows taskbar?',
    a: 'In "Replace" mode, the taskbar is smoothly hidden and set to auto-hide; Explorer is never terminated. When DockHub exits, crashes, or during logoff, your taskbar immediately returns to its original state. In "Both together" mode, the Windows taskbar is left completely untouched.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does DockHub require administrator privileges?',
    a: 'No. DockHub runs purely as a standard user process. Running as administrator is actually discouraged, because Windows prevents drag-and-drop between unelevated Explorer windows and elevated processes.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if the app crashes or is forcibly killed?',
    a: 'The built-in crash handler restores the taskbar before the process exits. In the event of a sudden power loss or force-kill via Task Manager, the taskbar remains in auto-hide mode and will slide open when your mouse hovers the screen edge. On your next session or by running "DockHub.exe --restore-taskbar", the original state is cleanly restored.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which Windows versions are supported?',
    a: 'Windows 10 1809 (build 17763) and higher. For rounded corners and Mica blur materials, Windows 11 22H2 or newer is recommended. The 60 MB installer bundles the .NET runtime, so there is nothing else to install.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it launch automatically on startup?',
    a: 'Yes. By default, the installer configures DockHub to start with Windows. You can easily toggle this off during installation or at any time in Settings › General.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do maximized windows cover the dock?',
    a: 'No. Like the Windows taskbar, the dock reserves its band of the screen: maximized windows stop at the dock at any display scaling and on every display that has a dock. DockHub rechecks it after display and Explorer changes and repairs it by itself.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I add widgets and change their appearance?',
    a: 'Right-click any empty space on the dock and select "Add widget", or browse the Widget Gallery in Settings and click the "+" button. To change a widget\'s style, right-click it directly on the dock and choose an option from the "Appearance" submenu.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do Win + 1…9 and the other taskbar shortcuts work?',
    a: 'Yes. Win + 1…9 and Win + 0 open, switch to or minimize the dock\'s first ten apps, with Shift for a new window, Ctrl + Shift to run as administrator and Alt for the jump list. Hold Win to see the numbers. You can also set your own shortcuts for showing the dock, the quick launcher, opening settings, pinning an app, auto-hide, volume and the microphone in Settings › Keyboard shortcuts.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the quick launcher?',
    a: 'A Spotlight-style search box that opens with Win + Alt + Space (or the search button, if you choose so in Settings › Taskbar). It finds apps, open windows, DockHub and Windows settings, recent files and dock commands, does quick math like 200*15%, and searches the web for anything else.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can a profile switch by itself?',
    a: 'Yes. A profile can switch in when a given number of displays is connected, while an app runs (for example steam or cs2), or during set hours, weekdays only if you like. When the app closes or the hours end, DockHub goes back to the profile you had.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I removed something by mistake. Can I get it back?',
    a: 'Yes. Click Undo on the notice next to the dock, choose Undo from the dock\'s right-click menu, or press Ctrl + Z in Settings. Widget data such as sticky notes is kept for 7 days, so it comes back too.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it use an internet connection?',
    a: 'Only for the features that need it: the weather widget and city search (Open-Meteo), exchange rates (Frankfurter, ECB data), your own calendar link, Todoist if you connect the To do widget, the AI usage widget with Claude Code (Codex and Gemini CLI are read from their local logs), and a once-a-day update check on GitHub that you can turn off in Settings › About. Web widgets can only reach the hosts they declare. No accounts, no telemetry, no tracking.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do updates work?',
    a: 'DockHub checks GitHub for a new release once a day. From Settings › About it downloads the installer, verifies its checksum and upgrades in place, keeping your settings.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where are my settings stored?',
    a: 'In %AppData%\\DockHub\\config.json, with notes, reminders and counters in the data subfolder. Files are written atomically, and DockHub keeps a backup from each of the last 7 days and loads the newest one by itself if the settings file is ever damaged. Settings › Backup and troubleshooting exports everything to a .zip you can import on another PC.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do the network, volume and battery icons work?',
    a: 'Yes. Windows 11 keeps its real icons inside Explorer, so since v0.7 DockHub draws its own. Scroll the volume icon to change the volume, middle-click to mute, right-click to switch the output device. The battery icon shows the charge and time left. Clicking them opens Windows\' Quick Settings, as does Win + A. Since v0.8 the tray also shows your keyboard language (click or scroll to switch), a microphone icon you can click to mute, and the number of waiting notifications next to the clock, with a moon while Do Not Disturb is on.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it support multi-monitor setups?',
    a: 'Yes. Turn on "Show on all displays" to get a dock on every monitor, each with its own size. The main display keeps your widgets and tray, running apps follow their windows, and docks come and go as you plug monitors in. A profile can even switch in when a given number of displays is connected.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is DockHub available in Turkish?',
    a: 'Yes. Choose Türkçe in Settings › General › Language. "System" follows your Windows display language.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I write my own widget?',
    a: 'Yes. A web widget is a folder with a manifest.json and an HTML page. It follows the dock\'s theme and gets settings, storage, notifications and a right-click menu through a small window.dockhub API. Share it as a .dockwidget file or simply a link (a manifest.json or a GitHub folder): users paste it in Settings › Widget gallery › Install from link and see its permissions before installing. The guide is docs/widget-sdk.md in the repository.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use it alongside StartAllBack or ExplorerPatcher?',
    a: 'It is not recommended. Running multiple third-party tools that hook or modify the Windows taskbar simultaneously can cause conflicts.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I install it with winget?',
    a: 'Once the package is accepted into the Windows Package Manager repository: winget install SametGurtuna.DockHub. Until then, use the installer from GitHub Releases.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is it completely free?',
    a: 'Yes. DockHub is free to download and use, and its full source code is on GitHub.',
  },
];
---

<section class="section" id="faq">
  <div class="wrap faq">
    <div class="faq-head reveal">
      <h2 class="section-title">Frequently asked questions.</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">Can't find the answer you're looking for?</p>
      <a class="btn btn-quiet faq-cta" href={site.issuesUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span set:html={icons.github} />
        Ask on GitHub
      </a>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-list">
      {
        faqs.map((f) => (
          <details class="qa reveal">
            <summary>
              <span>{f.q}</span>
              <span class="qa-ic" set:html={icons.plus} />
            </summary>
            <div class="qa-body">
              <p>{f.a}</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .faq {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.8fr) minmax(0, 1.2fr);
    gap: clamp(32px, 6vw, 96px);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .faq-head {
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
    justify-items: start;
    position: sticky;
    top: 100px;
  }
  .faq-head .section-title {
    max-width: 10ch;
  }
  .faq-cta {
    margin-top: 6px;
    --btn-h: 44px;
  }
  .faq-cta span {
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    display: grid;
  }
  .faq-list {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .qa {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    --reveal-y: 12px;
  }
  .qa summary {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 20px;
    padding: 22px 4px;
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.0625rem, 1.5vw, 1.1875rem);
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    border-radius: 6px;
    transition: color var(--t-fast);
  }
  .qa summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }
  .qa summary:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .qa-ic {
    flex: none;
    width: 30px;
    height: 30px;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--surface-hover);
    color: var(--text-2);
    transition:
      rotate var(--t-slow) var(--ease-out),
      background-color var(--t-fast),
      color var(--t-fast);
  }
  .qa-ic :global(svg) {
    width: 15px;
    height: 15px;
  }
  .qa[open] .qa-ic {
    rotate: 45deg;
    background: var(--accent);
    color: var(--accent-ink);
  }
  .qa-body {
    padding: 0 60px 24px 4px;
  }
  .qa-body p {
    color: var(--text-2);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }
  .qa::details-content {
    block-size: 0;
    overflow: clip;
    transition:
      block-size 380ms var(--ease-out),
      content-visibility 380ms allow-discrete;
  }
  .qa[open]::details-content {
    block-size: auto;
  }
  @supports (interpolate-size: allow-keywords) {
    :global(:root) {
      interpolate-size: allow-keywords;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .faq {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .faq-head {
      position: static;
    }
    .faq-head .section-title {
      max-width: 14ch;
    }
    .qa-body {
      padding-right: 8px;
    }
  }
</style>
